&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&

Dop is "distribution of privilege." It used in the following way: some
administrative programs (dxaccounts for example) notice when they are
being run by a non-root user. They then give you the option of being run
as root for "increased functionality." If you like that, you are asked to
provide the root password and the program comes up as root alongside your
other, normal applications. Dop is used in this process. If you disable
it, you won't be able to use this nifty feature.

There was a security loophole in dop. It has been closed in the more
recent versions of Digital Unix (I think it was closed in one of the patch
kits for DU v4.0B, but it might have been earlier than that). Most likely
you have a user who is trying to exploit that loophole. If that is so,
then you should be vigilant! Even if you are using a recent enough version
of dop to be safe, you may have a user who is trying to gain root access
on your machine. Expect other attempts in other ways!

*****************************************************************************
Peter
pchapin@twilight.vtc.vsc.edu            http://twilight.vtc.vsc.edu/~pchapin/

&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&&

