Thank you all for your answers.

I give the one from 
"Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-884-0646" <tpb@doctor.zk3.dec.com>

For all relatively current versions of Digital UNIX, you don't need to
build
a new kernel when you add memory or more CPUs of EXACTLY THE SAME TYPE
AS
YOU HAD BEFORE.  If you upgrade to a different CPU model and add CPUs at
the
same time, you will probably need to rebuild the kernel, but not because
you
added CPUs, because you changed the CPU model.


Daniel

From tru64-unix-managers  Wed Nov 18 09:44:51 1998
From: James Talbut <jet35 () phy ! cam ! ac ! uk>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 1998 09:44:51 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: NFS Locking being retained after process dies
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078728412005

The problem in brief, is that NFS clients are locking mail spool files, and keeping the locks even after the locking process dies.
Even deleting and recreating the file doesn't remove the lock (though it does free up the file, as the lock now points to the inode
for a nonexistant file).

I have had no definitive answers.

I have upgraded to the latest patches (2, on DU4.0D) and I haven't seen the problem since, but it doesn't happen very frequently so
I can't be sure that this has fixed it.

Thanks to:
  T.S. Horsnell
  Serguei Patchkovskii

for trying to help out.

